add("state-attorneys-general-cftc-swap-definition-rule-sports-event-contracts",
"43 State AGs Urge CFTC to Drop Sports Swap Rule","Regulatory",
"Attorneys general from 43 states and DC have told the CFTC it lacks authority and a quorum to treat sports event contracts as swaps.",
["united-states"],["ifrah-law-pllc","zwillgen-pllc","covington-and-burling-llp","greenberg-traurig-llp","fox-rothschild-llp"],"2026-11-22T07:30:00Z",[
"State law enforcement has opened a new front in the dispute over sports event contracts. On 18 November, the last day of the consultation period, attorneys general from 43 states and the District of Columbia filed two comment letters with the Commodity Futures Trading Commission. Both oppose the Commission's attempt to bring contracts on sports, political, cultural and weather events within the Commodity Exchange Act's definition of a swap. Only seven states declined to sign: Texas, Florida, Georgia, Indiana, Montana, New Hampshire and North Dakota.",
"The first letter, led by Ohio Attorney General Andy Wilson and co-signed by Kentucky, Maryland, Nevada, New Jersey, New York, Tennessee and Utah, targets the proposed rule titled Further Definition of Swap Inclusions, published in the Federal Register on 19 October 2026. The proposal would amend the swap definition so that event contracts on sporting outcomes sit squarely within the CFTC's exclusive jurisdiction. If finalised, prediction market platforms would have a much stronger argument that federal law pre-empts state gambling statutes, which is the central question in the litigation now running in several federal circuits.",
"The states make two main legal objections. The first is statutory: they argue Congress never gave the CFTC authority to redefine sports wagering as a financial derivative, and they point to the Commission's own 2024 statement that it is not a gaming regulator, as well as Sixth and Ninth Circuit decisions rejecting the CFTC-friendly reading of the Act. The second is procedural. The Commission currently has a single serving commissioner, Chairman Michael Selig, and the letter argues that a valid rulemaking requires a quorum of at least two and possibly four members. That point is not academic: a rule adopted without a lawful quorum would be exposed to challenge under the Administrative Procedure Act.",
"The letter also takes aim at consumer protection. It notes that the proposal contains none of the safeguards that state sports betting regimes require, such as age limits, self-exclusion and licensing. As a fallback, the attorneys general ask that any final rule apply only to contracts listed after it takes effect, which would limit its use as a defence in existing enforcement actions. The second letter, led by Maryland Attorney General Anthony G. Brown, addresses the interim final rule already in force, which excludes traditional sportsbook wagers and casino games from the swap definition. Maryland asks the Commission to widen that exclusion to cover all state-regulated gambling.",
"The level of interest in the docket shows what is at stake. Comments on the proposal rose from 11 on 9 November to 142 by 20 November, and on the interim rule from 7 to 124. Filers include Kalshi, Polymarket US, Coinbase, Cboe and the American Gaming Association. On 12 November President Trump nominated Nathan Anonick and Matthew Rowland to the Commission, and their confirmation would restore a quorum and remove one of the states' strongest procedural arguments. The CFTC has not said whether it will finalise or withdraw the proposal.",
"For licensed sportsbooks, tribal operators and state regulators, the practical lesson is that federal pre-emption remains unresolved and contested at every level: in rulemaking, in the courts and now in coordinated comment letters. Operators considering partnerships with prediction market platforms, or listing sports-related contracts themselves, should expect state attorneys general to keep pressing enforcement under state gambling law regardless of what the CFTC does. A final rule adopted before the quorum is restored would almost certainly be challenged immediately.",
"Market participants should review their exposure with US gaming counsel now. That includes mapping which states have taken enforcement action against event contract platforms, assessing how a final swap rule would interact with existing cease-and-desist orders, and preparing comment and litigation strategies for the next stage. Specialist gaming and derivatives lawyers can advise on how to structure products and partnerships while the boundary between federal commodities law and state gambling law is still being drawn."])

add("south-africa-national-online-gambling-tax-40-percent-effective-burden-treasury",
"South Africa's Online Gambling Tax: Is 40% Viable?","Tax",
"Treasury's proposed 20% national GGR tax on online gambling could push the effective burden to nearly 40%, operators warn.",
["south-africa"],["bird-and-bird","cms","pinsent-masons-llp","wh-partners"],"2026-11-22T08:45:00Z",[
"South Africa's National Treasury has moved its proposed national online gambling tax into a new phase of consultation. At a virtual stakeholder session held in mid-November, around 200 participants were given roughly ten minutes each to respond to the proposal for a 20 per cent national tax on the gross gambling revenue of online and interactive gambling. The proposal originates in Treasury's November 2025 discussion paper, The Case for a National Online Gambling Tax, and the Finance Minister confirmed in October's medium-term budget statement that a national gambling tax will be introduced, with its final design to be set out in the February 2027 budget.",
"The industry's main objection is cumulative burden. Licensed online operators already pay provincial gambling tax, typically around 6.5 per cent, and VAT on bets, which operators estimate amounts to an effective rate of about 12 per cent. Adding a 20 per cent national GGR levy on top could push the total effective tax rate to nearly 40 per cent. Treasury official Christopher Axelson, who leads the proposals, has described the measure as a sin tax, which suggests that Treasury sees it partly as a tool to curb consumption rather than only to raise revenue.",
"The legal structure of the tax matters as much as the rate. Gambling regulation in South Africa is a concurrent national and provincial competence, and provincial licensing boards already levy their own taxes on licensed operators. A national tax layered on top raises questions about how the two levels interact, whether provincial rates will be adjusted, and how revenue will be shared. Lawyer Wayne Lurie of Lurie Inc Attorneys has noted that the measure would be a money bill, requiring passage through the Finance Committees of the National Assembly and the National Council of Provinces, a vote in both houses and presidential assent. That process leaves room for amendments, but limited room for delay once the budget is tabled.",
"The black market is central to the policy debate. Yield Sec estimates cited in the consultation suggest that unlicensed operators may account for up to 62 per cent of the South African market. Operators argue that a near-40 per cent effective rate would widen the price gap between licensed and illegal sites, weakening channelisation at a time when enforcement against offshore operators remains limited. Industry participants, including Summit Media Africa's Jackee Kosen, have called on Treasury to publish an impact assessment and to model alternative rates before settling on 20 per cent.",
"There are also gaps in the wider regulatory framework that the tax debate has brought into focus. The national self-exclusion database contemplated by the National Gambling Act 2004 has still not been implemented, and online casino gaming remains outside the national framework even as provincially licensed online sports betting has grown rapidly. Critics argue that it is difficult to justify a sin tax on safer-gambling grounds while key harm-reduction tools are still missing. The Democratic Alliance reportedly opposed the increase at the session.",
"For operators and investors, February 2027 is the date to plan around. Businesses with South African licences should model the impact of a 20 per cent national levy on margins, bonus spending and marketing, and should assess whether contractual arrangements with suppliers, affiliates and platform providers allow costs to be shared or passed on. Those considering market entry should factor in the possibility that the final rate, base or deductibility rules may differ from the current proposal.",
"There is still time to influence the outcome. Operators and industry bodies that make detailed, evidence-based submissions on rate, base and interaction with provincial taxes are more likely to shape the final design. Specialist gaming tax counsel can help businesses prepare those submissions, assess constitutional and fiscal arguments, and plan compliance for the new regime once the budget is tabled."])

add("ksa-fake-regulator-website-typosquatting-dormant-dutch-licences-compliance",
"Fake KSA Site Exposes Dormant Dutch Licence Risk","Compliance",
"A one-letter copy of the KSA's website promoted black-market casinos for seven months, citing licences with no registered brands.",
["netherlands","malta"],["kalff-katz-and-franssen","stibbe","akd-benelux-lawyers","wh-partners"],"2026-11-22T10:00:00Z",[
"The Netherlands Gambling Authority, the Kansspelautoriteit (KSA), has reported a copycat version of its own website to the police and the national fraud help desk. The fake site, whose address differed from kansspelautoriteit.nl by a single letter, was registered on 10 April 2026 through the US registrar Namecheap and was used for about seven months to promote unlicensed gambling brands to Dutch consumers. The KSA has confirmed that the site has since been taken offline, has opened an internal investigation into how it went undetected for so long, and is working with a specialist partner on trademark infringement.",
"The scheme was uncovered after an article on 5 November on the affiliate site top10casino.nl about three new brands, Oranjester, De Oranje Vonk and Oranjekans. Registration data held by SIDN, the operator of the .nl registry, linked those brands to the fake regulator site. The site carried a guide presenting the brands as legitimate, naming JOI I and JOI II, two Malta-based companies licensed by the KSA in February 2022, and New Future Games BV, licensed in 2024, as the operators behind them. The KSA stated that no legal websites are currently being operated under those licences.",
"The case highlights a weakness in the Dutch licensing system: dormant licences. A Remote Gambling Act licence is publicly listed in the KSA register, but the licensee is not required to operate a brand immediately. That gives criminals a ready-made source of legitimacy. A consumer who checks the register will find the licensee's name and may assume that a site naming it is lawful. New Future Games' CEO, Paul van Dun, has confirmed that the KSA's account is accurate and that the company intends to activate its licence and launch a brand, BingoWorld, shortly.",
"For licensees, there are clear compliance and reputational issues. Licensees must notify the KSA of the domain names and brands they use, and only those notified sites may offer games of chance under the licence. A licensee whose name is misused has every incentive to act quickly: to alert the regulator, to take action against the infringing domains and to make clear on its own channels which brands are genuine. Silence could invite questions about whether the licensee had any connection to the infringing sites, and the KSA is likely to expect licensees to monitor misuse of their identity as part of their wider integrity obligations.",
"The incident also raises questions for the KSA itself. Typosquatting is a well-known technique, and the regulator's own domain was copied for more than half a year. The KSA's review will likely lead to more active monitoring of look-alike domains, faster cooperation with SIDN and registrars, and possibly a requirement that licensees with no active brands say so clearly in the public register. Similar issues have arisen in Belgium, where the Gaming Commission has warned about brand spoofing and fraudulent emails using its name.",
"Affiliates should take note as well. The scheme depended on publicity for the fake brands, and affiliate content is often the first place consumers encounter a new site. Dutch law prohibits advertising for unlicensed gambling, and the KSA has previously acted against affiliate websites. Affiliates should verify every brand against the KSA register's list of notified domains, not just the licensee's name, before publishing reviews or links.",
"Licensees, particularly those holding licences that are not yet in active use, should review their brand protection arrangements, domain monitoring and notification processes with Dutch counsel. Specialist gambling lawyers in the Netherlands and Malta can help licensees respond to identity misuse, coordinate with the KSA and registries, and pursue trademark and civil remedies against infringing operators."])

add("czech-republic-blockchain-crypto-casino-raid-betfin-europol-enforcement",
"Czech Raid on Blockchain Casino: Crypto Gambling Risk","Enforcement",
"Czech police and customs have charged 15 people over an unlicensed blockchain casino, showing decentralised gambling offers no legal shield.",
["malta","curacao"],["wh-partners","gvzh-advocates","camilleri-preziosi","cms"],"2026-11-22T11:20:00Z",[
"Czech authorities have dismantled an alleged organised group behind an unlicensed decentralised online casino, in one of the largest European enforcement actions to date against blockchain-based gambling. The Czech Customs Administration and the national police, supported by Europol and almost 300 officers, carried out more than 40 searches. Twenty people were detained and 15 have been charged. Three alleged principal organisers have been remanded in custody and face up to 13.5 years in prison if convicted. Authorities estimate the illicit benefit at around CZK 267 million, roughly EUR 10.9 million, and have seized assets worth about CZK 100 million.",
"According to reports, the platform, linked to the BETFIN project, used smart contracts and its own token and offered gambling to Czech players without authorisation from the Ministry of Finance, which licenses remote gambling under the Czech Gambling Act. Investigators analysed more than 2.3 million bets placed by around 28,500 players. The platform was promoted through social media, Telegram and Discord. The High Public Prosecutor's Office in Olomouc is supervising the case.",
"The legal significance lies in the rejection, in practice, of the argument that decentralised gambling falls outside national licensing regimes. Promoters of blockchain casinos often argue that because bets are settled by smart contracts, there is no operator in the traditional sense and therefore no one who requires a licence. Czech prosecutors have taken the opposite view: the people who designed, controlled, promoted and profited from the platform are treated as operators of unlicensed gambling, and the charges extend to organised criminal activity. That approach is consistent with how most European regulators interpret their legislation, which focuses on who offers and organises the game, not on the technology used to run it.",
"The case also shows how illegal gambling enforcement is moving beyond website blocking. The use of customs officers, police and Europol, combined with asset seizures and custodial remands, reflects the trend seen in the United Kingdom, the Netherlands and Germany towards following the money and targeting the individuals behind illegal operations. Tracing tokens and on-chain transactions is no longer beyond the capability of national authorities, and the public nature of many blockchains can make it easier, not harder, to reconstruct betting volumes and revenue flows.",
"For operators licensed in Malta, Curaçao and other hubs that accept cryptocurrency, the case is a reminder that a licence in one jurisdiction does not authorise offering services into another. Operators that market to Czech players through Telegram channels, Discord servers or influencer content without a Czech licence face real criminal exposure, not just administrative risk. Marketing activity aimed at a specific national audience is often the key evidence regulators use to show targeting.",
"Suppliers and payment intermediaries should also review their position. Game providers, token issuers, wallet providers and community managers can all be drawn into investigations where they are seen as part of an organised operation. Contracts with crypto-focused operators should include clear warranties on licensing and geo-restriction, audit rights and termination rights, and suppliers should keep records showing that they carried out due diligence on their partners.",
"Businesses operating crypto or token-based gambling products should obtain a jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction legal assessment of their market footprint, marketing channels and corporate structure. Specialist gambling lawyers can help operators decide where licensing is required, implement effective geo-blocking and know-your-customer controls, and respond quickly if authorities open an investigation."])

add("germany-ggl-q3-2026-stakes-online-slots-market-entry-signals",
"Germany Q3 Stakes Up 16%: Market Entry Signals","Market Entry",
"GGL data shows EUR 3.74bn in Q3 stakes and slot growth after stake limits were relaxed. What it means for operators eyeing Germany.",
["germany"],["hambach-and-hambach","cms-germany","redeker-sellner-dahs","bird-and-bird"],"2026-11-22T12:40:00Z",[
"New data from the Gemeinsame Glücksspielbehörde der Länder (GGL) gives the clearest picture yet of how Germany's licensed online market is responding to recent regulatory changes. Licensed stakes across sports betting, virtual slot games, online poker and online horse betting reached EUR 3.738 billion in the third quarter of 2026, a reported rise of 16 per cent. These figures are stakes, not gross gaming revenue, but they are the most reliable indicator of activity in the licensed channel that the GGL publishes.",
"The breakdown is instructive. Virtual slot stakes rose to EUR 1.442 billion, up from EUR 1.214 billion in the second quarter. Sports betting accounted for EUR 2.114 billion, of which EUR 1.634 billion was online and EUR 480 million was retail. Online poker fell slightly to EUR 150 million from EUR 158 million, and horse betting contributed EUR 32 million. Online channels make up about 87 per cent of the total. Stakes in the first quarter of 2026, at EUR 3.842 billion, were higher still, which reflects the seasonal effect of the football calendar on sports betting.",
"The growth in slot stakes is the figure that matters most from a legal perspective. Under a GGL guideline in force since 1 July 2026, the EUR 1 maximum stake per spin set by the Interstate Treaty on Gambling 2021 can be raised to EUR 3 for players aged 21 and over, and to EUR 5 for players who have shown no signs of problem gambling for 90 days. The increase in slot stakes in the first full quarter under the guideline suggests that the relaxation is pulling some play back into the licensed market, which was one of its stated aims.",
"The data should be read alongside the GGL's broader position. In its five-year review of the treaty, the regulator called for more flexibility and stronger direct powers against illegal online gambling, including measures targeting payment flows and internet service providers. Courts have also been examining the GGL's approach, with recent administrative court decisions raising questions about prohibition orders and the treatment of deposit-limit breaches under EU law. The federal states are expected to begin evaluating the treaty, and stake limits, tax rates and enforcement tools are all likely to be on the agenda.",
"For operators considering German market entry, the figures point in two directions. On the one hand, the licensed market is large and the regulator is showing willingness to adjust product rules where they push players offshore. On the other, Germany remains one of the most demanding regimes in Europe: virtual slot games are taxed at 5.3 per cent of stakes, a cross-operator EUR 1,000 monthly deposit limit applies through the LUGAS system, and table games such as online roulette and blackjack are licensed by individual states, not the GGL. Business cases need to be built on stake-based taxation, not GGR assumptions.",
"Operators already licensed in Germany should review how they have implemented the new stake tiers. Raising limits for players aged 21 and over, and again after 90 days without risk indicators, requires reliable age data, robust risk detection and documented decision-making. The GGL is likely to scrutinise how operators define the absence of problem-gambling signs, and inconsistent application could lead to supervisory action.",
"The coming treaty evaluation is an opportunity for licensed operators to make evidence-based arguments about channelisation, product rules and taxation. Specialist German gambling lawyers can advise on licensing applications to the GGL and state authorities, implementation of the stake-limit guideline, and engagement with the evaluation process, as well as defending prohibition orders and enforcement proceedings."])

